  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-b}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 4x-2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of the constant $b.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\sqrt{x-b} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1-b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(4x-2) \\\\  & =4(1)-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{1-b} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{1-b})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 1-b & =4 \\\\ b & =1-4 \\\\ b & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
